all dogs love to chew but what's a safe  chew toy to give your dog we check in  with dr jamie ogenstein the medical  director of oasis animal hospital in  henderson for answers  we have two very strong recommendations  one is  you should be able to take your  fingernail and create an indention  on what you're giving your dog to chew  and if you cannot do that  it is actually too hard and could  possibly break your dog's teeth and  people don't think it can happen but  trust me i've had pit bulls come in  with broken teeth and they're painful  the other recommendation we have is  to look on the treats if they're a good  treat that's for dental care  you can look for what's called the vohc  seals the veterinary oral health council  these are veterinarians veterinary  dentists who have come together and have  tested these products and they look for  certain things and once they say they're  good they put their stamp of approval on  it you can go to their website and  there's a list of all the things that  have their stamp of approval and those  are good things for them to chew on that  have been proven to help minimize tartar  i'm speaking of things that dogs like to  get their hands on of course scraps from  the table we've got thanksgiving right  around the corner  what should we avoid giving our pets  well and everybody likes to give things  off the table and i'm not one of those  veterinarians who tells you oh my god  that's a horrible thing to do i give my  dog treats but there's things to avoid  so obviously turkey bones  chicken bones those are bad the carcass  can actually cause problems the bones  can cause problems in the gi tract  um  the  grease drippings a lot of people like to  put a little on their their pet food  that can actually lead to pancreatitis  cases and they can end up in the  hospital very sick  same thing with if you have ham ham is a  very high fat meat um and then the bone  people like to give the ham bone  that can cause serious problems i've  seen dogs where they they  somehow swallow them and now you've got  an obstruction i even had a dog that it  got stuck  around this jar and couldn't get it off  we had to anesthetize that patient and  literally cut the bone off so be very  careful when you're giving bones
 

